page("how-to-season-a-compact-grill","How to Season a Carbon Steel Compact Grill","Season the raw 12-gauge carbon steel Slot Grill Compact so it cooks cleaner and resists rust: oven method, oils, Seasoning Balm at 400°F for 1 hour, and upkeep.",IMG["balm"],f"""
<p>The Slot Grill Compact is raw 12-gauge carbon steel, and so is the Slot Grill griddle (plancha). Like a cast-iron pan, raw steel does best with a seasoned coat: a very thin layer of oil baked on until it polymerizes and bonds to the steel. That layer gives you a natural non-stick surface and helps prevent rust. Do it once before your first cook, touch it up as needed, and the grill gets better with use.</p>
<h2>What seasoning actually is</h2><p>When a very thin film of oil is heated past its smoke point, the lighter elements burn off and what's left polymerizes into a hard, slick layer bonded to the steel. Slot Grill puts it this way for its <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a>: "It may not look like much is on there, but once heated, the lighter elements burn off and leave behind a polymerized layer that bonds to the steel." Thin is the key word. Thick coats stay sticky and flake.</p>
<h2>What you need</h2><ul><li><a href="{BALM}">Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a>, made for both Full Size and Compact Slot Grills, or a food-safe oil with a high smoke point</li><li>Paper towels or a scrap piece of cotton</li><li>An oven (the flat panels fit easily), or your grill fire</li><li>Dish soap, hot water and a cheap wire mesh scouring pad</li><li>Heat-safe gloves</li></ul>
<h2>Oven method (recommended)</h2><ol><li><b>Wash</b> each panel and grate with hot water, dish soap and a wire mesh pad to remove any shop residue and dust.</li><li><b>Dry completely.</b> Towel dry, then warm the pieces in the oven for a few minutes so any moisture evaporates.</li><li><b>Apply a very thin layer</b> of Seasoning Balm or oil to each plate. A light sheen is all you need. Wipe it on with a paper towel or cotton scrap, then wipe again as if you were trying to remove it all.</li><li><b>Bake at 400°F for 1 hour</b> to cure. That's Slot Grill's instruction for the Seasoning Balm. Place pieces flat or upright, and put a sheet of foil on the rack below to catch drips. Run the kitchen vent; it may smoke.</li><li><b>Let cool</b> in the oven.</li><li><b>Repeat as needed</b> to build up the coating over time. Two or three thin coats build a durable start.</li></ol>
<h2>On-the-fire method</h2><p>No oven? Assemble the grill, wipe a very thin coat on the cooking surfaces, build a hot charcoal fire, and let the steel heat until it stops smoking. Let cool, then repeat. It's less even than the oven but works, and every cook adds to it.</p>
<h2>What about the outside of the grill body?</h2><p>Slot Grill recommends high-temperature BBQ paint as a good option for the grill body, or the Seasoning Balm, which is also a good alternative to high-temp paint for seasoning the grill body. Unlike paint, the balm keeps everything food safe and consistent across the entire grill. <b>Never use BBQ paint on cooking surfaces or grates.</b></p>
<h2>What the finish will look like</h2><p>Seasoned carbon steel darkens over time from bronze to brown to black. It's normal for it to be uneven at first. Heat discoloration on the firebox is also normal. It doesn't need to look perfect to work.</p>
<h2>Keeping the seasoning</h2><ul><li>After each cook, scrape the grates and wipe on a light coat of oil while the steel is barely warm or once cool.</li><li>Cooking fatty foods (bacon, burgers) adds seasoning; acidic foods (tomato, citrus marinades) can wear it.</li><li>A wash with dish soap is fine on a polymerized coat; avoid soaking.</li><li>Always dry completely before storing.</li></ul>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Sticky or tacky surface</td><td>Coat too thick or not baked long enough</td><td>Bake again at 400°F; use thinner coats next time</td></tr><tr><td>Flaking</td><td>Thick coats or seasoning over rust</td><td>Scrub flakes off, dry, apply thin coats</td></tr><tr><td>Food sticks</td><td>New seasoning, or heat too low</td><td>Keep cooking and oiling; preheat well</td></tr><tr><td>Orange spots</td><td>Surface rust</td><td>See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a> and <a href="/how-to-re-season-a-carbon-steel-grill.html">re-seasoning</a></td></tr></table></div>
""","care",[("Do I need to season a Slot Grill?","It's recommended. The Slot Grill Compact is raw carbon steel, and a seasoned coat creates a natural non-stick surface and helps prevent rust."),("What temperature do I season a carbon steel grill at?","Slot Grill's instruction for its Seasoning Balm is to apply a very thin coat and bake at 400°F for 1 hour, repeating as needed."),("Can I use BBQ paint on my grill?","Slot Grill says high-temperature BBQ paint is an option for the grill body only. Never use BBQ paint on cooking surfaces or grates."),("Why is my seasoning sticky?","The coat was too thick or not heated long enough. Bake it again and use thinner coats.")])

page("how-to-clean-and-store-a-compact-grill","How to Clean and Store a Compact Grill","Between-cook cleanup, deep cleaning, and storing the flat-pack Slot Grill Compact so it stays rust-free: Slot Grill's recommended routine, step by step.",IMG["both02"],f"""
<p>Flat panels are the easiest grill parts in the world to clean. There are no hinges, no hidden corners and no ash pan to wrestle with: the Slot Grill Compact comes apart into flat pieces of 12-gauge carbon steel. Here's the routine Slot Grill recommends, plus how to store it so it's ready for the next trip.</p>
<h2>Right after cooking</h2><ul><li>While the grates are still warm (not hot), scrape off stuck food with a grill scraper or spatula.</li><li>Let the coals burn down. Never leave a fire unattended.</li><li>Wait until the ash and steel are completely cold. This can take hours.</li></ul>
<h2>Between cooks</h2><p>If you leave it assembled (under a carport or at a permitted campsite), you don't need a full clean every time. Once the ash is completely cold, pull one side panel, empty it, and slide the panel back in. Wipe the grates with a little oil.</p>
<h2>Deep clean</h2><ol><li>Let it cool completely and dump the ash into a metal container.</li><li>Disassemble: lift off the grates, slide out the side panels, pull the end pieces.</li><li>Wash with hot water, dish soap and a cheap wire mesh scouring pad (about $1.50).</li><li>Rinse and dry every piece completely with a towel. Warming the pieces over a small fire or in the oven drives off the last moisture.</li><li>Wipe a light coat of oil or <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a> on the grilling surfaces.</li></ol>
<h2>Cleaning the griddle</h2><ul><li>Scrape while warm.</li><li>For stuck-on food, add a splash of water to the warm griddle and scrape as it steams.</li><li>Wash with dish soap and a mesh pad if needed, dry completely, oil lightly.</li></ul>
<h2>Storing it</h2><p>Stack it flat, about 1" thick, somewhere dry. One owner keeps theirs under a bunk at work; others keep it in an overland trailer or RV closet. A bag or old box keeps soot off your gear.</p><blockquote>"Used it the first weekend we received it. Worked great, easy cleanup and it easily stored in our overland trailer."<cite>Verified Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Long-term storage</h2><ul><li>Deep clean, dry completely and oil all cooking surfaces before storing for a season.</li><li>Store indoors or in a dry shed, off concrete floors that wick moisture.</li><li>Avoid sealed plastic bins with residual moisture; add a desiccant pack if you use one.</li><li>Check and re-oil every month or two in humid climates.</li></ul>
<h2>Dealing with ash</h2><p>Cold ash goes in a metal can with a lid. Never put warm ash in plastic, paper or the trash. Follow local rules for ash disposal.</p>
<h2>If you see rust</h2><p>Light surface rust is cosmetic. Scrub it off with a cheap wire mesh pad, dry, re-oil, and heat. Thick steel shrugs that off for years. See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a> and <a href="/how-to-re-season-a-carbon-steel-grill.html">re-seasoning</a>.</p>
""","care",[("How do I clean the Slot Grill Compact?","Once cold, empty the ash, disassemble, wash with hot water, dish soap and a wire mesh pad, dry completely, and wipe the grilling surfaces with oil or Seasoning Balm."),("Do I need to clean it after every cook?","No. If you leave it assembled, just empty the cold ash by pulling one side panel and sliding it back, and oil the grates."),("How should I store a carbon steel grill?","Clean, completely dry and lightly oiled, stacked flat in a dry place. A bag or box keeps soot off other gear."),("Is surface rust a problem?","Light surface rust is cosmetic. Scrub it off, dry, re-oil and heat.")])

page("rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill","Rust Prevention for a Carbon Steel Grill","Keep a raw carbon steel Slot Grill rust-free: why steel rusts, daily habits, storage, humid and coastal climates, and what to do when rust appears.",IMG["balm"],f"""
<p>Raw carbon steel is what makes the Slot Grill tough: 12-gauge on the Compact, 10-gauge on the Full Size. It's also why it needs a little care. Unprotected carbon steel rusts when it meets moisture and oxygen. The good news is that preventing rust takes about a minute after each cook, and thick steel is very forgiving if you slip up.</p>
<h2>Why carbon steel rusts</h2><p>Iron in the steel reacts with water and oxygen to form iron oxide (rust). Salt, humidity, rain, dew, acidic foods and wet ash speed it up. A seasoned or oiled surface puts a barrier between the steel and the air.</p>
<h2>The four habits that stop rust</h2><ol><li><b>Season it.</b> A polymerized oil layer is your main protection. See <a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">how to season</a>; Slot Grill's <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a> is made for this and helps prevent rust.</li><li><b>Dry it completely.</b> Every time it gets wet, towel dry and warm it until all moisture is gone.</li><li><b>Oil it after use.</b> Wipe a very thin coat of oil or balm on the cooking surfaces once cool.</li><li><b>Store it dry.</b> Indoors or in a dry shed, never in standing water or wet grass.</li></ol>
<h2>Don't store ash in the grill</h2><p>Ash absorbs moisture and becomes corrosive when wet. Empty cold ash after every cook, especially before rain or storage.</p>
<h2>The grill body</h2><p>Slot Grill recommends high-temperature BBQ paint as a good option for the grill body, or the Seasoning Balm as a food-safe alternative that keeps everything consistent. <b>Never use BBQ paint on cooking surfaces or grates.</b></p>
<h2>Humid, rainy and coastal climates</h2><ul><li>Re-oil more often; check every few weeks in storage.</li><li>After beach or river trips, rinse off salt and grit, dry thoroughly and oil.</li><li>Don't leave it assembled outside in the rain.</li><li>Use a breathable bag rather than a sealed plastic bin, or add a desiccant pack.</li></ul>
<h2>What to do when rust appears</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Rust level</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td>Orange haze or spots</td><td>Scrub with a wire mesh pad, dry, oil, heat</td></tr><tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Rough, flaky patches</td><td>Scrub hard with a mesh pad or steel wool, wash, dry, then re-season. See <a href="/how-to-re-season-a-carbon-steel-grill.html">re-seasoning</a></td></tr><tr><td>Heavy</td><td>Thick scale</td><td>A wire brush, then full re-season. Thick steel can take it.</td></tr></table></div><p>Light surface rust is cosmetic. Thick steel shrugs that off for years.</p>
""","care",[("Will my Slot Grill rust?","Raw carbon steel will surface-rust if left wet or unoiled. Seasoning, drying, oiling after use and dry storage prevent it."),("How do I remove rust from a carbon steel grill?","Scrub with a wire mesh pad or steel wool, wash, dry completely, then oil and heat or re-season."),("Should I leave ash in the grill?","No. Ash absorbs moisture and gets corrosive. Empty it once cold after each cook."),("Can I paint my grill?","Slot Grill says high-temperature BBQ paint is an option for the grill body only, never cooking surfaces or grates. The Seasoning Balm is a food-safe alternative.")])

page("how-to-re-season-a-carbon-steel-grill","How to Re-Season a Carbon Steel Grill or Griddle","Strip rust or flaky seasoning and rebuild a durable coat on a Slot Grill or griddle: scrub, dry, thin coats, bake at 400°F for 1 hour, repeat.",IMG["pl1"],f"""
<p>Sometimes seasoning needs a reset: after a grill sat outside through a wet season, after rust, after a thick sticky coat started flaking, or when a griddle has worn patchy from acidic foods. Re-seasoning carbon steel is simple, and with thick steel like the Slot Grill's there's nothing to damage.</p>
<h2>When to re-season</h2><ul><li>Rust that goes beyond a light haze</li><li>Flaking or peeling seasoning</li><li>Sticky, gummy surfaces</li><li>Food sticking badly on the griddle despite oiling</li></ul>
<h2>What you need</h2><ul><li>Wire mesh scouring pad, steel wool or a wire brush</li><li>Dish soap and hot water</li><li>Towels</li><li><a href="{BALM}">Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a> or a high-smoke-point oil</li><li>Paper towels or a cotton scrap</li><li>Oven (or a hot grill fire)</li></ul>
<h2>Step by step</h2><ol><li><b>Disassemble</b> the grill into flat panels.</li><li><b>Scrub</b> away rust and flaky seasoning with a mesh pad, steel wool or a wire brush. You don't need bare shiny steel everywhere; just remove anything loose.</li><li><b>Wash</b> with hot water and dish soap to remove rust dust and debris.</li><li><b>Dry completely</b> and warm the pieces in the oven a few minutes so no moisture remains. Re-seasoning over moisture brings rust right back.</li><li><b>Apply a very thin coat</b> of Seasoning Balm or oil to every surface you want to protect, then wipe off nearly all of it. A light sheen is enough.</li><li><b>Bake at 400°F for 1 hour</b>, following Slot Grill's balm instructions. Use foil on a lower rack.</li><li><b>Cool and repeat</b> 2–3 times for a durable base coat.</li><li><b>Cook something fatty</b> first, like bacon or burgers on the griddle, to keep building it.</li></ol>
<h2>Re-seasoning on the fire</h2><p>Without an oven, assemble the grill, apply thin coats, and heat over a hot fire until the smoking stops. Repeat several times.</p>
<h2>Avoid</h2><ul><li>Thick coats (they turn sticky).</li><li>Painting cooking surfaces or grates. BBQ paint is for the grill body only, per Slot Grill.</li><li>Seasoning over rust or moisture.</li></ul>
""","care",[("How do I re-season a carbon steel griddle?","Scrub off rust and flakes, wash, dry completely, apply very thin coats of oil or Seasoning Balm, and bake at 400°F for 1 hour, repeating 2 to 3 times."),("Do I need to get to bare metal?","No. Remove anything loose or rusty; solid seasoning can stay."),("Why did my seasoning flake?","Usually thick coats or seasoning applied over rust or moisture."),("Can I re-season without an oven?","Yes. Apply thin coats and heat the assembled grill over a hot fire until it stops smoking, repeating several times.")])
